\s15\qc \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch \ab\af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \b\fs52\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 Lubavitch Jewish Center
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\qc \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \ab\af0 \ltrch \b\fs48\insrsid2562004 Bar Mitzvah Guidelines

\par }{\rtlch \ab\af0 \ltrch \b\fs36\insrsid2562004 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch \ab\af0 \ltrch \b\fs28\insrsid2562004 Introduction}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \fs28\insrsid2562004 
\par }{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 
\par At Lubavitch Jewish Center, our primary goal is preparing our children to live Jewish lives with pride, with knowledge, and with enthusiasm.  When your child reaches the age of Bar or Bat Mitzva
h, we share with your family the privilege and responsibility of welcoming him into the adult Jewish community.  Together, we hope to instill the meaning and significance of the occasion, to celebrate appropriately, and to honor your child's place among t
he Jewish people.  At Lubavitch Jewish Center we have prepared the following guidelines to assist you in planning for this joyous occasion.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch \ab\af0 \ltrch \b\fs28\insrsid2562004 Who can be Bar Mitzvah at Lubavitch?
\par }{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 
\par In order to hold your child's ceremony and/or celebration at Lubavitch, the following requirements must be met:
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \af0\alang1025 \ltrch \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls2\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 
Your child must be born to a Jewish mother or converted under Orthodox auspices.  If the mother or maternal grandmother was converted to Judaism, please set up an appointment with the Rabbi to determine if those conversio
ns met with the Chabad standards for conversion.  According to the Talmud, religious identity is determined solely by the mother and an intermarried family where the father is not Jewish has no effect on the religious identity of the child.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \af0\alang1025 \ltrch \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls2\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 
We now require a commitment of continued Jewish education of at least one year }{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \ul\insrsid2562004 following the Bar/Bat Mitvah.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 
  This may be in the form of your child continuing studies in a Jewish Day School or through his participation in our Sunday morning teen division.  We believe tha
t this type of commitment underscores our message that the Bar/Bat Mitvah is the beginning and not the end of a lifetime of Jewish learning, commitment, and spiritual fulfillment.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \af0\alang1025 \ltrch \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}Upon receiving a date for the Bar/Bat Mitzvah, we require the family to join
 our synagogue and attend a minimum of twice a month for Shabbat services.  This includes becoming a member of our synagogue for a minimal fee of $613.  To join, a simple form needs to be filled out.  
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch \ab\af0 \ltrch \b\insrsid2562004 
\par 
\par }{\rtlch \ab\af0 \ltrch \b\fs28\insrsid2562004 Age and Dates}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 
\par 
\par Bar Mitzvah is the day on which a chi
ld becomes responsible for Mitzvot and accountable for both positive and negative behaviors under Jewish law.  According to the Talmud, this takes place at as they each develop and mature, at age 13. This happens automatically on their Jewish birth date, 
r
egardless of the way the day is marked.  The ceremony and celebration should take place as close to the Jewish birthday as possible.  The Jewish calendar is lunar and represents the years from creation.  The secular calendar is solar and represents Christ
ian years.  As a child grows, the Jewish and secular birthdays may move weeks apart. To determine your child's Jewish birthday, a two hundred year calendar must be consulted with the Rabbi's guidance.  
\par 
\par We recommend scheduling Bar Mitzvahs at least 18 mont
hs prior to your child's 13th birthday. While planning and preparation takes about one year, it is important to get the date on the Chabad calendar to avoid any conflicts.  To choose a date, set up an initial meeting with the Rabbi to determine your child
's Jewish birthday.  Your child's Bar Mitzvah must be in the year following that date, as close as possible to the date but not before it.  
\par 
\par 
\par }{\rtlch \ab\af0 \ltrch \b\fs28\insrsid2562004 Bar Mitzvah ceremony and preparation
\par }{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 
\par The Bar Mitzvah ceremony takes place on Saturday morning from 9:30am - 12:15
pm and is following by a Kiddush that usually ends about 1:00.  Men and women are seated separately for the traditional service but are joined later for the Kiddush luncheon.  The service includes a morning Shabbat service from 9:30 -10:30, the Torah read
i
ng from about 10:30-11:20, speeches by the Bar Mitzvah boy and the Rabbi, and the concluding service from about 11:50 - 12:15.  If you have not been to a service at Chabad, please come and participate in our intimate, traditional, and lively Shabbats.  At
 each Bar Mitzvah, we are delighted with the wonderful comments from guests about the warmth and spirituality of the service.
\par 
\par A boy begins Bar Mitzvah preparation classes 1 year prior to his Bar Mitzvah.  Classes are private, and are set up directly with t
he Rabbi.  Classes are held once a week for 15 minutes to a half hour at a mutually convenient time. There is a $750 fee for Bar Mitzvah tutoring and all other preparations that may be invovled. Tefillin will need to be purchased before the Bar Mitzvah an
d can be ordered from New York by a scribe who offers us wholesale prices.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 Classes with the Rabbi will include the following:
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \af0\alang1025 \ltrch \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls3\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 
Discussions about the meaning and significance of becoming a Bar Mitzvah.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \af0\alang1025 \ltrch \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls3\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 
Learning how to put on tefillin, the accompanying blessings, its meaning, makeup, and relevance.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \af0\alang1025 \ltrch \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}Studying the Haftorah reading, Torah reading, accompanying blessings, the meaning and relevance of the portions read during the ceremony.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \af0\alang1025 \ltrch \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 4.\tab}Assistance in the writing of the Bar Mitzvah speeches, and "Dvar Torah."
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \af0\alang1025 \ltrch \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.\tab}Optional study for the leading of part of Shabbat services.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 
\par }{\rtlch \ab\af0 \ltrch \b\fs28\insrsid2562004 
\par Celebrating the Bar Mitzvah
\par 
\par }{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 There are several ways to celebrate this special occasion and honor your child.  Families may choose to celebrate over the weekend with a party on Saturday or Sunday nig
ht or a Sunday brunch. The party must be one at  which  the food that is brought in meets our Kashrut standards.  In addition, we offer the following suggestions for celebrating at Lubavitch.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \af0\alang1025 \ltrch \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls5\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 
Shabbat services should be followed by a Kiddush in honor of the
 Bar Mitzvah child.  The Kiddush can be light refreshments or a buffet lunch and the menu is open as long as our Kashrut standards are met.  Our regular worshippers will join in the Kiddush so when planning, don't forget to include an extra 40-50 guests.

\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \af0\alang1025 \ltrch \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls5\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 L
ubavitch approves certain caterers, meats, and bakeries. Please speak to Rabbi Shemtov or Weinstein for ideas and suggestion in this regard.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid2562004 
\par 
\par For additional information or to schedule a Bar Mitzvah, please call Rabbi Weinstein at 215-497-9925.
\par  
